          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.3,
      "why": "iterative produced 0 feasible points in 33 evals, likely because its N+3 cycle latency (N~10-14 means 13-17 cycles/result) requires Fmax >= 130-170 MHz for 1 MSPS, and the shared datapath may not reach that Fmax at the widths needed for 2^-10 accuracy. However, iterative has the smallest area per rotation (single datapath, no k-way parallelism), so if it can meet throughput at all, it could dominate on luts_plus_ffs. I keep a small share to probe whether any iterative config clears the throughput constraint at the minimal area, which would expand the Pareto front toward the low-area end. If it remains infeasible, the budget is cheap insurance."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The fleet facts show unrolled_k is my workhorse: 40 feasible points, best area 415, and 15 of 19 merged-front designs. iterative has been infeasible so far (0/33), likely a throughput wall. The spec's binding constraints are throughput >=1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10. For unrolled_k, the key trade-off is k (throughput vs area) and n_iter/data_width (accuracy vs area). I focus 70% of budget on unrolled_k with tight ranges around the known-feasible region (W 12-16, N 10-14, k 2-4, minimal guards) to densely map the Pareto front in the area/accuracy space. The remaining 30% probes iterative at the same accuracy settings to check if any low-area point sneaks through the throughput constraint, which would extend the front to lower luts_plus_ffs. Both families compute identical bits, so accuracy tuning is shared; the difference is purely in the datapath structure and resulting Fmax/area."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is established and the primary objective (min luts_plus_ffs) is satisfied by the `iterative` family design with 273 LUTs+FFs, which is well below the HV reference of 1500. The constraint `max_abs_err <= 0.000976562` is met by all 3 designs on the front (best 0.000389). The constraint `throughput_msps >= 1` is met by all designs (best 15.6). The front covers the trade-off space adequately for the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs), which picks the 273 LUTs+FFs design. Since this is the final round and the front is stable with a clear winner for the selection rule, stopping is appropriate."
}
